I pushed two additions and split the bullet in three, since it had grown longer than its neighbours.

**Reporting the attempt both ways.** Your version reports it when the attempt fails. The other direction matters just as much: when the existing API does cover the case, the close should carry the working snippet, not the refusal alone. #63477 and #64749 both closed with runnable code, and that is what makes the answer read as help rather than as a no.

**Changed behavior, not only additions.** The riskier half is modifying public API that already exists, because nobody opts into it. Three cases from the same day: #61786 changed `MermaidDumper::dump()`, shipped in 7.4, to return an empty string; #64779 changed `Callback` to honour a `false` return; and a rework of my own on #60505 made the feature depend on a header that only exists when the toolbar is enabled.

The detector for it is @derrabus', on #64779, and it is cheap enough to be a rule: *"This PR changes some old callback fixtures to make existing tests pass and that's a pretty good indicator that we've broken BC."*

The file is identical on 6.4, 7.4, 8.1 and 8.2, so the merge up stays trivial.
